      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town became a resort destination in the 1830s when James Patton built the 350‑room Warm Springs Hotel, its 13 columns symbolizing the original colonies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Springs%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1882 the railroad arrived, prompting Rumbough to enlarge his hotel and making Hot Springs a rail‑connected getaway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Springs%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A higher‑temperature spring was discovered in 1886, leading the town to change its name from Warm Springs to Hot Springs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Springs%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During World War I, the town’s former hotel grounds were leased to the U.S. government and used as an internment camp for enemy aliens.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Springs%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The historic Dorland Memorial Presbyterian Church, built in 1886, sits on Bridge Street and is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
